From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Graham Ollis <plicease@cpan.org>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 09:00:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] str_gets(): a 512-byte buffer, and keep NUL bytes

Without STDSTDIO (patch 0002), str_gets() reads a line a character at a
time into a static 8 KB buffer, then copies it into the string, looping
for longer lines. On DOS that buffer was 8 KB of the 64 KB DGROUP, which
the stack shares (docs/M1-REPORT.md, section 4; issue #9). Under MSDOS
it is now 512 bytes; lines longer than that take more passes of the
existing loop.

The copy used str_set() and str_cat(), which stop at a NUL byte, so a
line read with <FH> from a binary file lost everything after its first
NUL. Under MSDOS it now copies by length (str_nset(), str_ncat()), as
the STDSTDIO code does.
---
 str.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/str.c b/str.c
index 7ec76fe..ecb5aaa 100644
--- a/str.c
+++ b/str.c
@@ -863,7 +863,11 @@ thats_really_all_folks:
 #else /* !STDSTDIO */	/* The big, slow, and stupid way */
 
     {
+#ifdef MSDOS
+	static char buf[512];	/* DGROUP is scarce; longer lines loop */
+#else
 	static char buf[8192];
+#endif
 	char * bpe = buf + sizeof(buf) - 3;
 
 screamer:
@@ -875,10 +879,18 @@ filler:
 	    goto filler;
 
 	*bp = '\0';
+#ifdef MSDOS
+	/* by length, so a line with a NUL in it is kept whole */
+	if (append)
+	    str_ncat(str, buf, bp - buf);
+	else
+	    str_nset(str, buf, bp - buf);
+#else
 	if (append)
 	    str_cat(str, buf);
 	else
 	    str_set(str, buf);
+#endif
 	if (i != newline && i != EOF) {
 	    append = -1;
 	    goto screamer;
